  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 226 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Our anticipated warm, moist pattern from a Bermuda High \nand Hudson Bay upper low has arrived in full swing. Yesterday's \nthunderstorm activity was not quite as widespread as anticipated, \nbut what did develop produced very heavy rainfall rates along the \nfoothills of the Adirondacks, especially along Ticonderoga, NY east \ninto Shoreham, VT. For today, another hot and humid day is expected, \nalthough guidance is suggesting more appreciable dry subsidence this \nafternoon. As a result, dewpoints appear to mix out from the upper \n60s to lower 70s, down into the mid 60s (maybe even lower 60s) \nduring the afternoon. So it appears like we will just avoid needing \nheat headlines again, even with toasty mid 80s to lower 90s across \nthe region. With the drier conditions aloft, thunderstorm coverage \nshould be minimal. We lack any forcing, and so mainly a couple spot \nshowers or storms will be possible on eastern slopes of the \nAdirondacks positioned where it interfaces with the summertime lake \nbreeze off Lake Champlain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: For tonight, a compact, but relatively vigorous trough \nwill lift northeast. The warm, humid air will be capable of \nproducing nocturnal convection, with 1000-1500 J/kg of mid-level \nCAPE. This could make for some nighttime rumbles of thunder heading \ninto Saturday. This trough will amble east by Saturday morning. , \nbut another trough hot on its heels will result in some scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms once again. The axis of the trough slides \nthrough Saturday night, and so a few lingering showers will be \npossible heading into Sunday. Before more stable air arrives, \ninstability will begin to increase after sunrise, and so a few \nthunderstorms may spark across eastern Vermont during the day on \nSunday. Overall, though, Sunday will be dry for much of the area.\n\nBehind the system on Sunday, there is a pocket of drier air that \nwill filter in. Our dewpoints should briefly fall below 60 during \nthe day. If clouds ahead of the next system can hold out long \nenough, then we may be able to radiate out for somewhat more \ncomfortable sleeping conditions Sunday night into Monday. There's on \nlast surge of warm, humid weather ahead of another trough coming \nthrough Monday afternoon and evening. Vorticity embedded within the \nupper low will dig southeast, and start driving cooler towards our \narea. This will result in a strengthening frontal boundary ahead of \ndeveloping low pressure near Montreal. The focusing mechanism and \nincreasing shear may make Monday a day to watch for some stronger \nstorms before we make the transition to cooler, but still showery \nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The start of next will feature seasonably warm \nconditions, although less humid compared to previous days as the \nBermuda high weakens and westerly flows prevails across the region. \nHighs at the start of the week will be in the upper 70s to mid 80s, \nbut by the middle of the week they will drop a few degrees to be in \nthe lower 70s and low 80s. A cold front looks to move through the \nregion sometime late Monday into Tuesday, bringing widespread \nshowers and a few possible thunderstorms, although the timing of the \nboundary will have a large influence on any convective elements. \nAdditional showers may be possible towards the middle of next week, \nalthough there still plenty of uncertainty at this time frame.",
